  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nFollowing a beautiful fall day on Saturday, another mainly dry\nand seasonable day is expected today, though there will be an\nincrease in clouds this afternoon associated with a weak area \nof low pressure tracking northeastward off the East Coast. A few\nshowers on the northwestern periphery of this system could \nreach some areas south and east of Albany this afternoon. Any\nprecipitation amounts would be rather light on the order of a\nfew hundredths to around 0.10 of an inch.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA strong cold front associated with a potent upper level and \nsurface low over Quebec will cross the region late tonight\nthrough tomorrow morning. Some rain showers will likely \naccompany the front and mainly impact the Adirondacks and \nwestern Mohawk Valley tonight. In the wake of these showers, \nrapidly cooling temperatures aloft (850 hPa temperatures falling\nto near to below 0C) will allow for a brief lake effect \nresponse with some lingering showers for the same areas Monday \nmorning before ending. Winds will pick up behind the front with \nfrequent gusts 25 to 35 mph expected on Monday with the \nstrongest winds likely within the Mohawk Valley to the Capital \nDistrict and Berkshires. Winds on Tuesday will not be as strong\nas on Monday with gusts mainly 15 to 25 mph. High temperatures \nby Tuesday will run 5 to 10 degrees below normal with most areas\nonly reaching the 50s except the mid- Hudson Valley which \nshould reach the lower 60s. Lows will reach the 30s to lower 40s\neach night. Some patchy frost will be possible for parts of the\nHudson Valley both Monday and Tuesday night with the higher \nchances north of Albany. However, there may be enough surface \nwind to prevent frost formation on Monday night. Temperatures \nrebound back toward normal values for the mid to late week \nperiod. A developing longwave trough could allow for a few \nshowers at times later this week, mainly for areas north and \nwest of Albany, but no impactful weather is expected at this \ntime.",
